      "body": "Rapidus is promising 2nm-class wafers at roughly $20,000 when volume production starts in 2027, well below TSMC's reported price. The number is real enough to matter, but the hard part is still yield, customers and time.\nAtsuyoshi Koike has put a price on Japan's semiconductor comeback. According to Bloomberg's Japanese report carried by TBS on July 8, 2026, the Rapidus president said the company would use 3 million to 3.5 million yen per wafer as a reference price for its 2nm-class chips. At recent exchange rates, that is roughly $18,500 to $21,500. TrendForce, citing Nikkei and Business Post, said TSMC's 2nm and Intel's 18A wafers have been reported around $30,000. That's the boast. Rapidus wants to come in nearly $10,000 cheaper than the foundry everyone else still has to measure against.\nDon't miss the awkward part. TSMC isn't just expensive because it can be. Counterpoint Research put TSMC at 73% of pure-play foundry revenue in the first quarter of 2026, while Samsung sat at 7% an",
