      "summary": "TSMC advances chipmaking with A16 and new backside power technology Sammy Fans",
      "body": "TSMC has developed a new technology, A16, that could make future chips faster, smaller, and more power-efficient. The company has successfully tested the technology and plans to begin mass production in the fourth quarter of 2026.\nThe main improvement in A16 is a technology called backside power delivery. In traditional chips, power lines and signal lines are placed on the same side. As chips become smaller, there is less space for these lines. This creates congestion and can cause power loss.\nTSMC solves this problem by moving the power lines to the back of the chip. Special connections send electricity directly to the transistors. This leaves more space on the front side for signal connections.\nA major advantage of TSMC’s method is that it can work with many existing chip designs. Designers will not have to completely change the way they create chips. This could save time and reduce design costs.\nTSMC says A16 can provide 8 to 10% higher performance while using the same amount of pow",
